rows('precursors',[
('zinc-stock','Zinc acetate stock','The typical preparation uses 90 mL of 0.101 M Zn(OAc)₂·2H₂O in DMSO. This is the undoped reference stock; in doped preparations the zinc concentration is reduced to keep total starting metal concentration constant. A separate exact dopant mass is not reported for every characterization specimen.',2,'II.A Sample Preparation',['zno-route','co-route','ni-route']),
('base-stock','Tetramethylammonium hydroxide stock','The stated base is N(Me)₄OH·5H₂O dissolved in ethanol. The typical batch receives 30 mL of 0.552 M base, described as 1.8 equivalents of OH⁻. The pentahydrate is retained; no anhydrous reagent or independently measured water content is substituted.',2,'II.A Sample Preparation',['zno-route','co-route','ni-route']),
('cobalt-source','Cobalt precursor','Co(OAc)₂·4H₂O is added to the zinc precursor solution for Co²⁺ doping. Initial feed percentages, overall measured composition, deliberately surface-bound cobalt and spectroscopically internal cobalt are distinct quantities. No universal conversion from feed to internal dopant fraction is established.',2,'Doping procedure',['co-route']),
('nickel-source','Nickel precursor','Ni(ClO₄)₂·6H₂O is the experimentally named nickel salt. Although Figure 4 uses a generic TM(OAc)₂ notation, it does not override the explicitly stated nickel perchlorate precursor. Exact nickel charges for individual spectroscopic samples are not supplied.',2,'Doping procedure',['ni-route']),
('dmso','Dimethyl sulfoxide','DMSO is the principal room-temperature synthesis medium and an optional redispersion solvent. The discussion contrasts this procedure with earlier ethanol synthesis in reference 13; that earlier recipe is not imported as another experiment from this source.',2,'Sample preparation and introduction',['zno-route','co-route','ni-route']),
('ethanol','Ethanol','Ethanol dissolves the base, washes particles, supports optional redispersion and supplies the concentrated Co:ZnO suspension used for aggregation. It also serves as an antisolvent during TOPO processing and as the medium of the SI luminescence comparison. These roles do not establish a shared physical batch.',2,'Sample preparation',['zno-route','co-route','ni-route','aggregation','topo-zno','topo-co','topo-ni']),
('antisolvents','Ethyl acetate and heptane','Either ethyl acetate or heptane may precipitate particles from DMSO. Washing then uses ethyl acetate. The source gives alternatives rather than a prescribed ethyl-acetate/heptane mixture; volumes and wash counts are absent.',2,'Isolation procedure',['zno-route','co-route','ni-route']),
('clarity-reagent','Optional zinc-acetate addition','Approximately 10 mg Zn(OAc)₂ is reported to restore optical clarity to a cloudy ZnO suspension. Hydration, initial particle concentration and final composition for this optional intervention are not specified. It is not automatically applied to all source specimens.',2,'Optical clarity restoration',['clarity-restoration']),
('dodecylamine','Dodecylamine','Dodecylamine precipitates washed nanocrystals from DMSO and provides an intermediate capped state before TOPO processing. Its charge is missing. The source stresses removing excess Zn²⁺ first because amine otherwise causes rapid growth and poorer uniformity.',2,'TOPO preparation',['topo-zno','topo-co','topo-ni']),
('topo','Technical-grade trioctylphosphine oxide','TOPO from Aldrich is used at 180 °C, and 1 mg additional TOPO accompanies final redispersion. The bulk treatment charge is not reported. Note 16 says technical material contains phosphonic acids and suggests, without directly establishing ZnO ligand identity, that these may play a ligating role analogous to the cited CdSe case.',2,'TOPO preparation and note 16',['topo-zno','topo-co','topo-ni']),
('toluene','Toluene and other nonpolar solvents','Dodecylamine-capped particles are redispersed in toluene before repeated ethanol precipitation. TOPO-treated particles may be dispersed in several unspecified nonpolar solvents; achieved ZnO concentration in toluene exceeds 0.4 g mL⁻¹. This is a reported achievable concentration, not a charge fixed for every batch.',2,'TOPO preparation',['topo-zno','topo-co','topo-ni']),
('analysis-materials','Analytical supports and auxiliary materials','Optical work uses quartz cuvettes or quartz disks, helium cooling and frozen-solution glasses. Co:ZnO TEM specimens are deposited from ethanol onto graphite supports. These are analytical preparation contexts; the LaB₆ electron source is an instrument component, not a ZnO precursor.',3,'II.B Physical Measurements',['microscopy','optical-absorption','mcd','zeeman']),
('stock-conflict','Base concentration and equivalent conventions','The typical recipe gives 0.552 M base. Figure 1a and associated prose use 0.554 M, while Figure 1b caption gives 0.552 M and its prose gives 0.554 M. The stated 30 µL into 250 µL of 0.101 M metal corresponds by curator arithmetic to about 0.658 equivalent, whereas Figure 1a calls it 0.6; Figure 4 and SI S1 instead state 0.66. These are preserved as source-specific conditions, not silently reconciled.',3,'Figure 1 and Results',['pure-kinetics','pure-titration','dopant-series'],'curator_interpretation','source_evidence_conflict'),
])
more('analysis-materials',5,'Figure 5 specimen preparation');more('stock-conflict',4,'Figure 4 caption');more('stock-conflict',1,'Figure S1 caption','si')
rows('protocol',[
('alkaline-growth','Room-temperature alkaline hydrolysis and condensation','Add the ethanolic base dropwise at approximately 2 mL min⁻¹ to the DMSO metal solution under constant stirring at room temperature. The typical undoped batch uses the 30 mL/90 mL stocks above. Temperature in degrees, vessel geometry, stirring speed, pressure and a final holding time are not specified.',2,'II.A Sample Preparation',['zno-route','co-route','ni-route'],'method_context'),
('dopant-substitution','Dopant addition at constant total metal','Introduce cobalt acetate tetrahydrate or nickel perchlorate hexahydrate into the initial zinc solution and decrease zinc concentration to preserve total metal concentration. This defines the doped synthesis framework but does not uniquely specify every final measured dopant concentration or a batch-specific incorporation yield.',2,'Doping preparation',['co-route','ni-route'],'method_context'),
('workup','Precipitation, washing and redispersion','Precipitate the DMSO colloid with ethyl acetate or heptane, wash with ethyl acetate and redisperse in DMSO or ethanol. Absolute workup volumes, centrifugation settings, number of washes, isolated yield and final concentration are absent.',2,'Particle isolation',['zno-route','co-route','ni-route'],'method_context'),
('clarity','Optional clarification','Add approximately 10 mg zinc acetate to a cloudy nanocrystalline ZnO suspension to restore clarity, as stated by the source. No explicit duration, temperature, mechanism or quantitative turbidity endpoint is given.',2,'Optical clarity sentence',['clarity-restoration'],'method_context'),
('remove-zinc','Remove excess zinc before amine processing','The source explicitly removes excess Zn²⁺ precursor before adding dodecylamine. This constraint avoids amine-triggered rapid growth at the expense of size uniformity. An analytical endpoint and removal efficiency are not reported.',2,'TOPO preparation',['topo-zno','topo-co','topo-ni'],'method_context'),
('amine-workup','Dodecylamine exchange and solvent removal','Precipitate washed particles from DMSO by adding dodecylamine, then wash with ethanol to remove DMSO. Redisperse the dodecylamine-capped particles in toluene and precipitate with ethanol twice. Neither amine dose nor solvent volumes are supplied.',2,'TOPO preparation',['topo-zno','topo-co','topo-ni'],'method_context'),
('topo-heating','Heating in TOPO','Heat the resulting powder in TOPO at 180 °C for at least 30 min. The minimum duration is a bound, not an exact universal dwell. Heating ramp, TOPO quantity and treatment atmosphere are not specified.',2,'TOPO preparation',['topo-zno','topo-co','topo-ni'],'method_context'),
('topo-cooling','Cooling and post-treatment washing','Cool TOPO-treated particles below 80 °C, then precipitate and wash with ethanol. The cooling threshold is strict as printed; cooling rate, additional wash count and separation settings remain unreported.',2,'TOPO preparation',['topo-zno','topo-co','topo-ni'],'method_context'),
('topo-redispersion','Final nonpolar colloids','Redisperse with 1 mg additional TOPO in a nonpolar solvent. Toluene dispersions above 0.4 g mL⁻¹ are reported. The description of indefinite stability has no measured storage interval and is not converted into a numeric shelf life.',2,'TOPO preparation',['topo-zno','topo-co','topo-ni'],'method_context'),
('aggregation','Room-temperature aggregation','Allow solvent to evaporate from a concentrated Co:ZnO suspension in ethanol in air on a benchtop at room temperature. No elevated-temperature annealing or subsequent aggregation treatment is used. Concentration, evaporation time, humidity and aggregate yield are unspecified.',2,'Aggregation preparation',['aggregation'],'method_context'),
('pure-kinetics','Early formation kinetics','Figure 1a follows spectra every 30 s between 30 s and 10 min after adding 30 µL of 0.554 M base to 250 µL of 0.101 M zinc solution in DMSO. The source labels this 0.6 equivalent; the arithmetic discrepancy is retained. The first spectrum already shows the ZnO band edge.',3,'Figure 1a and Results',['pure-kinetics'],'method_context'),
('pure-titration','Stepwise hydroxide titration','Figure 1b uses successive 0.2-equivalent base aliquots. The plotted series reaches 2.0 equivalents; no titration interval or raw spectral table is supplied. Caption and prose differ between 0.552 and 0.554 M, so the reader retains both rather than selecting one.',3,'Figure 1b and Results',['pure-titration'],'method_context'),
('co-titration','Cobalt-incorporation titration','The Figure 3 series uses an initial 98% Zn/2% Co acetate mixture and 0.2-equivalent base titration under otherwise comparable conditions. Color photos and ultraviolet/visible spectra track the same source titration. A sample bottle does not establish a precisely resolved coordination compound.',4,'Figure 3',['co-titration'],'method_context'),
('dopant-series','Dopant-concentration comparison','Figure 4 adds 0.66 equivalent of 0.554 M base to 250 µL of a 0.101 M total-metal DMSO solution. The plotted nonzero metal fractions are 2, 4, 6 and 8%; each response is averaged over three independent measurements. Solid lines fit nonzero-concentration points; dashed segments guide the eye.',4,'Figure 4 and text',['dopant-series'],'method_context'),
('thermal-ripening','DMSO heating series','Figure 1c follows ZnO in DMSO at 50 °C after 2, 5, 20, 40 and 120 min. The author interpretation is thermally accelerated Ostwald ripening. This separate aging series is not the 180 °C TOPO surface-cleaning stage.',3,'Figure 1c',['thermal-ripening'],'method_context'),
('room-aging','Room-temperature DMSO aging','The undoped ZnO cohort in Figure 2 was aged in DMSO for two days at room temperature. The broader observation of growth over days or weeks does not replace this specific two-day specimen history.',7,'IV.A and Figure 2 discussion',['room-aging'],'method_context'),
('si-kinetics','Supporting kinetic comparison','SI Figure S1 compares pure ZnO with 2% added Co²⁺ or 2% added Ni²⁺ after 0.66-equivalent base addition to 250 µL of 0.101 M DMSO zinc solution. The plotted time axis is minutes, and solid lines are single-exponential fits. Numerical fit time constants are not supplied.',1,'Figure S1',['pure-kinetics','dopant-series'],'method_context','reported_source_method','si'),
('surface-control','Deliberately surface-bound cobalt control','SI Figure S2 measures particles with cobalt intentionally bound at the surface before TOPO treatment and after 30 and 120 min. This control demonstrates signal removal but is not the internally doped 1.7% Co spectroscopic sample or a complete independently specified surface-loading recipe.',2,'Figure S2',['surface-cleaning-control'],'method_context','reported_source_method','si'),
('microscopy-method','TEM, electron diffraction and SEM acquisition','High-resolution TEM and electron diffraction use a JEOL 2010 at 200 kV with a high-brightness LaB₆ filament at PNNL. SEM uses a Serion XL-30 field-emission instrument, preserving the printed name. For as-grown Co:ZnO imaging, ethyl-acetate precipitation and ethanol resuspension precede deposition onto graphite TEM supports.',3,'II.B Physical Measurements',['microscopy'],'method_context'),
('xrd-method','Powder X-ray diffraction','A Philips PW 1830 diffractometer is specified. Selected Scherrer results agree with optical size estimates, and aggregate/nonaggregate patterns are said to match. The paper provides no standalone raw XRD figure, scan settings or refined CIF.',3,'II.B Physical Measurements',['diffraction'],'method_context'),
('icp-method','Dopant analysis by ICP-AES','Dopant concentrations are measured using inductively coupled plasma atomic-emission spectrometry on a Jarrel Ash model 955. Digestion, calibration, uncertainties and an initial-feed-to-final-dopant mass balance are not supplied for every cohort.',3,'II.B Physical Measurements',['icp'],'method_context'),
('absorption-method','Room-temperature absorption and optical sizing','A Varian Cary 5E records absorption, using 1.0 cm quartz cuvettes for room-temperature colloids. Routine mean diameters derive from empirical tight-binding bandgap–size relations in references 17 and 18, checked against Scherrer analysis and selected TEM specimens. Optical sizes remain model-derived rather than independent direct measurements.',3,'II.B Physical Measurements',['optical-absorption'],'method_context'),
('lowtemp-absorption','Low-temperature sample preparation','Absorption at 7 K uses TOPO-treated particles spin-coated on quartz disks or frozen-solution glasses cooled by helium vapor in a Janis STVP-100 continuous-flow cryostat. The two forms are alternatives tied to individual samples; a film is not automatically a colloidal measurement.',3,'II.B Physical Measurements',['optical-absorption'],'method_context'),
('mcd-method','Magnetic circular dichroism acquisition','MCD uses the same spectroscopic samples in an Aviv 40DS-based UV/visible/NIR instrument with a CryoIndustries SMC-1659-OVT superconducting magneto-optical cryostat in Faraday geometry. Its stated capability is below 1.5 to 300 K and 0–7 T; those are instrument ranges, not the conditions of every curve.',3,'II.B Physical Measurements',['mcd'],'method_context'),
('mcd-definition','MCD reporting convention','The source defines ΔA = A_L − A_R for left- and right-circularly polarized light and reports θ(degrees) = 32.9 ΔA/A. The source-specific normalization is retained. Equation 1 separately defines a C₀/D₀ intensity ratio, whereas equation 2 defines B₀/D₀.',3,'II.B MCD definition',['mcd','mcd-intensity-model'],'method_context'),
('zeeman-method','Band-edge Zeeman acquisition','Band-edge Zeeman data are acquired on the same instrument in single-beam transmission, using Abs = −log(I/I₀). Figure 9b is the 1.7% Co:ZnO frozen-solution cohort at 5 K, not the Ni film or aggregate powder.',3,'II.B Physical Measurements',['zeeman'],'method_context'),
('magnetometry-method','SQUID magnetometry','Variable-temperature and variable-field magnetic susceptibility is measured on Co:ZnO powders with a Quantum Design MPMS SQUID. Sample-holder diamagnetism is subtracted from raw data. The source does not supply the sample mass, full background trace or every sweep-rate setting.',3,'II.B Physical Measurements',['magnetometry'],'method_context'),
('luminescence-method','Supporting luminescence measurement','SI Figure S4 compares absorption and emission from pure ZnO and 2% Co:ZnO suspensions in ethanol at 300 K. Excitation wavelength, spectrometer, slit settings and detector-response correction are not supplied, so no complete fluorescence acquisition protocol is reconstructed.',3,'Figure S4',['luminescence'],'method_context','reported_source_method','si'),
])
