rows('structures',[
('pure-tem','Aged undoped ZnO dimensions','Figure 2 describes nearly spherical, crystalline ZnO after two days of DMSO aging. A histogram of approximately 100 particles gives 3.8 ± 0.6 nm; the optical estimate is 3.8 nm and the bandgap maximum is 29 500 cm⁻¹. The histogram does not supply individual particle coordinates or counts as a data table.',3,'Figure 2 discussion',['microscopy','room-aging']),
('pure-diffraction','Undoped ZnO electron diffraction','Figure 2c is wide-area electron diffraction, assigned to highly crystalline wurtzite ZnO. Indexed labels are 112, 103, 110, overlapping 101/002 and 102. Figure 2a and 2d scale bars are 20 nm and 5 nm respectively. No new zone axis or measured lattice spacing is inferred.',4,'Figure 2',['microscopy','diffraction']),
('co-tem','As-grown cobalt-doped ZnO dimensions','The approximately 5% Co:ZnO cohort in Figure 5 is as-grown from DMSO. The overview shows about 30 particles; analysis of approximately 100 isolated particles gives 2.9 ± 0.3 nm, compared with an optical estimate of 2.8 nm. It is distinct from later 1.7% Co and 3.6% Co specimens.',5,'Figure 5 and associated text',['microscopy']),
('co-diffraction','Cobalt-doped ZnO crystallinity','Figure 5c supports wurtzite ZnO with labels 112, 103, 110, overlapping 101/002 and 102. The particles are described as nearly spherical and highly crystalline. Overview and high-resolution scale bars are 20 nm and 5 nm; no quantified polymorph fraction or site-by-site dopant map is supplied.',5,'Figure 5',['microscopy','diffraction']),
('ni-tem','Supporting Ni:ZnO microscopy','SI Figure S3 supplies a TEM image with a 200 nm scale bar and an electron-diffraction pattern for TOPO-treated Ni:ZnO. The SI does not state a matching 1.5% composition, particle-size histogram or individual diffraction indices for this image. Those details are not inherited from the separate optical-film cohort.',2,'Figure S3',['microscopy','diffraction'],'source_cohort','reported_source_fact','si'),
('aggregate-morphology','Co:ZnO aggregate morphology','The Figure 10 inset shows dense micron-scale gel-like aggregates with a 50 µm SEM scale bar. The text calls the nanocrystal diameter 5.0 nm on page 6 and 4.9 nm on page 13, at 3.6% Co. Both source values are retained, without claiming distinct measured distributions.',7,'Figure 10 inset',['aggregation','microscopy'],'curator_interpretation','source_evidence_conflict'),
('aggregate-phase','Diffraction before and after aggregation','The authors report identical powder XRD patterns for aggregated and nonaggregated samples and no new phase detected. The patterns are not plotted here. This is a source-reported observation with an unspecified detection limit, not an independently quantified bound on possible minor phases.',13,'IV.D.v',['diffraction','aggregation']),
('dopant-homogeneity','Spectroscopic evidence for internal substitution','Resolved ligand-field structure after cleaning is interpreted as homogeneous, substitutional Co²⁺ or Ni²⁺ in wurtzite ZnO. Surface-bound dopants have different coordination and are removed through TOPO solvation. The source does not supply a spatial dopant map, per-particle composition or measured atomic occupancy coordinates.',9,'IV.C and IV.D.i',['topo-co','topo-ni','optical-absorption'],'model_context','author_interpretation'),
('cleaning-structure','Surface cleaning and possible size change','TOPO treatment removes surface-dopant signatures and may improve spectral homogeneity relative to the cited isocrystalline-core/shell process. If particles were not already ripened in DMSO, the high temperature also accelerates ripening and increases diameter. Surface cleaning is therefore not assumed to preserve every specimen size.',9,'IV.C',['topo-co','topo-ni','surface-cleaning-control'],'model_context','author_interpretation'),
('lattice-context','Unit-cell dimensions used in exchange discussion','The exchange model cites ZnO lattice parameters a = 3.2495 Å and c = 5.2067 Å to explain strong orbital overlap. These are contextual lattice dimensions, not a new refinement of each doped nanocrystal. No complete measured CIF, dopant-supercell coordinates or surface-ligand geometry is supplied.',12,'Exchange discussion',['exchange-model'],'cited_context','literature_context'),
('structure-boundary','Crystal-model boundary','Original TEM, HRTEM and electron-diffraction images are preserved. Diffraction assignments and literature lattice constants do not justify fabricating a unique Co/Ni site distribution or an exact ligand-coated nanocrystal. Any later atomistic model must identify its external structural source and modeling assumptions.',13,'Full structural evidence',['source-context'],'curator_interpretation','reviewed_source_limitation'),
])
more('pure-tem',7,'Two-day DMSO aging');more('aggregate-morphology',6,'Results diameter 5.0 nm');more('aggregate-morphology',13,'Discussion diameter 4.9 nm')
rows('properties',[
('pure-formation','Early ZnO absorption evolution','The Figure 1a band edge grows, narrows and redshifts over the first ten minutes, after which a metastable state is reached. The source interprets the narrowing as size-distribution focusing. Subsequent room-temperature growth occurs on days-to-weeks scales; neither a numeric kinetic constant nor a raw spectrum matrix is given.',3,'Figure 1a and Results',['pure-kinetics']),
('pure-base-response','Hydroxide-dependent ZnO formation','The first 0.2-equivalent addition does not establish ZnO nucleation. At 0.4 equivalent the source reports small, inhomogeneous ZnO, a band near 32 500 cm⁻¹ and characteristic green trap luminescence. A pseudospheres-based optical estimate gives about 2 nm; at 0.6 equivalent the 30 000 cm⁻¹ band gives about 3.2 nm.',7,'IV.A and Figure 1b',['pure-titration']),
('pure-intensity','Absorbance beyond nucleation','Early titration intensity changes are irregular. Above roughly one equivalent, bandgap absorbance increases approximately linearly with added hydroxide, with little further energy shift. The source interprets this as continued nucleation/growth rather than growth of all existing particles beyond approximately 3.2 nm.',3,'Figure 1b discussion',['pure-titration']),
('thermal-response','Spectral response to heating','The 50 °C DMSO series shows lower bandgap energy and a slow intensity decrease over 2–120 min. The authors attribute this to particle growth by Ostwald ripening, without a fitted diffusion coefficient, activation energy or directly measured time-dependent diameter table.',3,'Figure 1c',['thermal-ripening']),
('co-color','Cobalt coordination and color','The Figure 3 solution changes from pink to blue. The starting weak absorption centered near 19 000 cm⁻¹ is associated with pseudo-octahedral Co²⁺, while structured visible absorption is assigned to tetrahedral lattice Co²⁺. The final doped bandgap near 33 000 cm⁻¹ is higher than the approximately 30 000 cm⁻¹ undoped comparison.',4,'Figure 3 and Results',['co-titration']),
('co-intermediate','Intermediate and internal cobalt signals','Up to 0.3 equivalent base there is no evident Co spectral change; near 0.35 equivalent a broad band centered around 17 500 cm⁻¹ develops, rises to about 0.6 equivalent and then decreases. Above about 0.45 equivalent a structured band with central peak around 16 500 cm⁻¹ grows. The discussion later describes the broad intermediate near 19 000 cm⁻¹; both descriptions remain source-scoped.',4,'Figure 3 discussion',['co-titration'],'curator_interpretation','source_evidence_conflict'),
('co-isosbestic','Isosbestic and decomposition evidence','Figure 3 has isosbestic points near 18 550 and 20 750 cm⁻¹. Figure 11 uses single-value decomposition to separate bandgap, intermediate and internal-Co contributions; intermediate and internal intensities are plotted ×60. The source compares the deconvolved intermediate with cobalt in DMSO plus excess acetate and with TOPO-treated internally doped particles.',7,'Figure 11',['co-titration']),
('dopant-energy','Dopant-dependent bandgap energies','At fixed base addition, the Co series shows an approximately linear bandgap-energy increase with initial Co concentration. Introducing Ni also raises the energy, but further increases above approximately 1% produce little additional shift. This is a metastable synthesis response, not a measured intrinsic bulk bandgap-versus-doping law.',4,'Figure 4a and text',['dopant-series']),
('dopant-number','Dopants inhibit apparent nanocrystal formation','Bandgap intensity decreases as dopant feed increases. The authors infer approximately 35% fewer nanocrystals at 2% added Co or Ni and fit slopes of −9% nucleation per % Co and −6% per % Ni. Initial concentration changes are steeper. A base-only control has about twentyfold smaller effect, arguing against simple base consumption as the sole cause.',8,'Figure 4 interpretation',['dopant-series','nucleation-model'],'model_context','author_derived_interpretation'),
('si-kinetic-results','Supporting kinetic curves','SI S1 plots bandgap energy and absorbance versus minutes for pure ZnO, 2% added Co and 2% added Ni. Doped traces have higher energy and lower absorbance than the pure reference. The displayed single-exponential curves are fits; no fitted rate constants or dense time-series values are invented.',1,'Figure S1',['pure-kinetics','dopant-series'],'source_cohort','reported_source_fact','si'),
('co-spectra','Co:ZnO low-temperature spectra','Figure 6a is a frozen solution of 5 nm, 1.7% Co:ZnO: absorption at 7 K and MCD at 5 K over 0–6.5 T. The structured ⁴A₂→⁴T₁(P) ligand-field band is centered near 15 900 cm⁻¹. Integrated oscillator strength is 2.0×10⁻³; the cited bulk value is 5.0×10⁻³, not another current colloidal measurement.',5,'Figure 6a and text',['optical-absorption','mcd']),
('co-ct-band','Co:ZnO sub-bandgap response','The Co absorption edge tails into the visible without a distinct low-temperature inflection. A broad negative MCD feature increases toward about 25 200 cm⁻¹; overlap with the positive bandgap signal means this is a lower limit for its maximum. Uniform field/temperature scaling supports a common magnetic chromophore across regions.',5,'Figure 6a and charge-transfer discussion',['optical-absorption','mcd']),
('ni-spectra','Ni:ZnO low-temperature spectra','Figure 6b uses a thin film of TOPO-treated 4.0 nm, 1.5% Ni:ZnO: absorption at 7 K and variable-field MCD at 5 K. The source defines ligand-field, charge-transfer and bandgap regions as 15 160–16 300, 19 400–25 300 and 26 000–28 000 cm⁻¹ respectively. Different sample thicknesses were needed for the weak bandgap response.',5,'Figure 6b and text',['optical-absorption','mcd']),
('mcd-relative','Relative MCD strengths','The Co bandgap C₀/D₀ ratio is approximately one hundredfold smaller than the ligand-field ratio. The Ni bandgap rotational strength is approximately an order of magnitude smaller than Co, and its bandgap data are poorer than the lower-energy regions because identical film thickness could not be used throughout.',5,'MCD intensity discussion',['mcd','mcd-intensity-model']),
('table1-co','Table 1: Co intensity ratios','For Co:ZnO, C₀/D₀ and energy pairs are 0.36 at 15 432 cm⁻¹ (ligand field), approximately 0.081 at approximately 25 000 cm⁻¹ (charge transfer), and 0.0032 at 28 000 cm⁻¹ (bandgap). Footnote a explicitly limits the precision of the charge-transfer values because of overlapping bandgap intensity.',6,'Table 1',['mcd-intensity-model']),
('table1-ni','Table 1: Ni intensity ratios','For Ni:ZnO, B₀/D₀ and energy pairs are 0.057 at 15 163 cm⁻¹ (ligand field), 0.016 at 23 000 cm⁻¹ (charge transfer), and 0.00088 at 28 000 cm⁻¹ (bandgap). These B-term ratios are not interchangeable with the Co C-term ratios.',6,'Table 1',['mcd-intensity-model']),
('fine-structure','Resolved ligand-field spectra','Figure 7 expands the same Co and Ni samples used in Figure 6, with absorption at 7 K and MCD at 5 K, 7 T. Some lines have widths as small as 30 cm⁻¹. For Co the fine energies agree with cited bulk spectra to approximately 1 cm⁻¹, supporting weak confinement effects on these localized transitions.',6,'Figure 7 and IV.D.i',['optical-absorption','mcd']),
('ni-fine','Nickel fine structure','The source assigns the Ni ³T₁(F)→³T₁(P) band primarily to a trigonally split T₂ spinor component. A₁ and E features occur at 15 163 and 15 198 cm⁻¹ with approximately 30 cm⁻¹ widths and opposite MCD signs. At higher energies overlapping electronic and vibronic transitions broaden the spectrum.',9,'IV.D.i',['optical-absorption','mcd']),
('fano','Fano antiresonance','Note 25 identifies a prominent MCD antiresonance dip at 17 700 cm⁻¹. It distinguishes a weaker absorption dip at the same energy previously attributed to a ²A₁(G) state in reference 20. The two statements are retained without inventing a fitted Fano parameter.',9,'Note 25',['optical-absorption','mcd']),
('field-co','Co field and temperature response','Figure 8a shows Co ligand-field and bandgap signals saturating at high field and low temperature, consistent with C-term behavior. Co curves include 5 and 10 K; the bandgap energy is 27 200 cm⁻¹. The caption states 15 600 cm⁻¹ for the ligand-field signal, whereas prose states 15 700 cm⁻¹.',6,'Figure 8a and accompanying prose',['mcd'],'curator_interpretation','source_evidence_conflict'),
('field-ni','Ni field and temperature response','Figure 8b shows Ni signals at 5, 10, 20, 40 and 80 K, with labeled fields from 1 to 6 T. Signals do not saturate at high field and are approximately temperature independent below about 40 K. The caption states 15 400 cm⁻¹, while prose states 15 165 cm⁻¹. The inset is an energy-level model, not a measured diffraction structure.',6,'Figure 8b and accompanying prose',['mcd'],'curator_interpretation','source_evidence_conflict'),
('zeeman-results','Co excitonic Zeeman shift','For the Figure 6a Co cohort at 5 K, Figure 9b reports an average redshift of approximately 53 cm⁻¹ T⁻¹ over 0–7 T. The field response is nonlinear, turns between 1 and 3 T and has an approximate slope of 65 cm⁻¹ T⁻¹ between 3 and 7 T. Lines represent model predictions, not extra measured data.',6,'Figure 9b and Results',['zeeman']),
('magnetic-loop','Aggregate hysteresis at 300 K','The 3.6% Co:ZnO aggregate powder shows a 300 K loop with coercivity 105 Oe, remanence 0.465×10⁻³ emu g⁻¹ and saturation moment 2.56×10⁻³ emu g⁻¹. These values belong to the aggregate powder and cannot be assigned to dispersed 1.7% Co nanocrystals.',6,'Figure 10 results',['magnetometry']),
('curie-bound','Magnetic ordering above the measured range','Coercivity and remanence vary only weakly with temperature, and no Curie transition is observed through the instrument limit of 350 K. The source therefore states T_C > 350 K. This is a lower bound, not a transition measured at 350 K or a general claim for all Co:ZnO preparations.',7,'Figure 10 and IV.D.v',['magnetometry']),
('magnetic-fractions','Coexisting magnetic components','At low temperature, ferromagnetic and paramagnetic signals coexist in the aggregate, indicating substantial magnetically isolated cobalt. The authors estimate that about 10% of the 10 K magnetization is ferromagnetic and infer a 300 K moment near 0.3 μ_B per cobalt, or 10% of 3 μ_B per cobalt. They caution that this may overestimate the value because antiferromagnetic or spin-glass contributions can be magnetically silent.',13,'IV.D.v',['magnetization-model'],'model_context','author_derived_estimate'),
('si-cleaning-results','Loss of surface-cobalt absorption','SI Figure S2 retains the original broad absorption before TOPO treatment and its progressive decrease after 30 and 120 min. The source interprets the decline as removal of deliberately surface-bound Co. No quantitative surface-coverage fraction or total cobalt mass balance is reported.',2,'Figure S2',['surface-cleaning-control'],'source_cohort','reported_source_fact','si'),
('quenching','Co-induced luminescence quenching','At 300 K, adding 2.0% Co is reported to quench more than 99% of ZnO surface-trap luminescence. SI S4 shows the corresponding absorption/emission comparison in ethanol. The source attributes quenching to deep trapping and rapid nonradiative relaxation through ligand-field states; no absolute quantum yield or measured lifetime is provided.',9,'IV.D.i and SI S4',['luminescence']),
('no-lf-emission','Emission-scope limit','No upper excited-state ligand-field emission is observed from these colloids at room temperature. Note 29 instead cites bulk Co:ZnO ⁴T₁(P) emission at cryogenic temperatures below 5 K with low quantum yields; that is external literature context, not a new colloidal acquisition.',9,'IV.D.i and note 29',['luminescence'],'cited_context','source_and_literature_scope'),
('si-lf-comparison','TOPO, core/shell and bulk comparison','SI Figure S5 compares room-temperature ligand-field absorption from a bulk single crystal, the earlier isocrystalline-core/shell method of reference 13 and a blue trace labeled TOPO cleaned 5%. This current 5% label does not establish identity with the as-grown Figure 5 TEM cohort, the 1.7% low-temperature sample or the 3.6% aggregate.',3,'Figure S5',['topo-optical-comparisons'],'source_cohort','reported_source_fact','si'),
('si-bandgap-comparison','Bandgap before and after TOPO treatment','SI Figure S6 compares approximately 4.2 nm undoped ZnO before and after TOPO processing at room temperature. The curves are labeled DMSO synthesis and TOPO capped in toluene. The rising absorbance around 35 000 cm⁻¹ is attributed to toluene, not another ZnO transition.',4,'Figure S6',['topo-optical-comparisons'],'source_cohort','reported_source_fact','si'),
])
more('co-intermediate',8,'Intermediate center in IV.B');more('fine-structure',9,'Bulk comparison');more('quenching',3,'Figure S4','si');more('curie-bound',13,'T_C interpretation')
rows('intuition',[
('motivation','Why prepare magnetic semiconductor colloids','The authors seek chemically controllable diluted magnetic semiconductors for spin-based electronics and magneto-optics. The introduction discusses spin LEDs, low ordering temperatures in earlier materials and contradictory thin-film ferromagnetism reports. Defects, carriers and segregated metals are competing explanations in cited work, not all experimentally resolved by this synthesis.',1,'Introduction',['source-context'],'cited_context','literature_context'),
('colloid-rationale','Why use solution chemistry','Solution processing is proposed to improve control of dopant speciation, provide scalable preparation and make nanocrystals processable building blocks. The DMSO procedure is presented as enabling gram quantities in open containers without special precursors, air exclusion or precise temperature stabilization; no isolated mass yield or manufacturing study is supplied.',2,'Introduction and conclusion',['source-context'],'model_context','author_rationale'),
('dopant-problem','Why distinguish surface and internal dopants','Surface-exposed dopants can differ from substitutional ions in coordination, ligation and electronic exchange. The source stresses both controlling incorporation and experimentally establishing dopant distribution rather than assuming homogeneous doping from nominal feed composition alone.',2,'Introduction',['nucleation-model'],'model_context','author_rationale'),
('basic-acetate','Proposed prenucleation clusters','Initial hydroxide additions are interpreted as forming basic zinc-acetate molecular clusters before ZnO appears. Zn₄O(OAc)₆ and Zn₁₀O₄(OAc)₁₂ are examples established in earlier ethanol studies; analogous DMSO clusters are proposed, not isolated and structurally measured in the present experiment.',7,'IV.A and reference 22',['nucleation-model'],'cited_context','author_interpretation'),
('nucleation-threshold','Exclusion from initial nuclei','In Figure 11, internal-Co intensity extrapolates to zero near 0.36 equivalent base, close to ZnO nucleation, and has no initial jump coincident with the bandgap. The authors infer that initial critical nuclei exclude Co quantitatively and that incorporation proceeds nearly isotropically during subsequent growth. This is a mechanistic inference from spectroscopy, not a direct atom-counting map.',8,'IV.B and Figure 11',['nucleation-model'],'model_context','author_interpretation'),
('intermediate-model','Proposed surface intermediate','The intermediate’s broad spectrum, higher ligand-field energy and comparison with cobalt plus excess acetate suggest heterogeneous, surface-associated tetrahedral Co. Before nucleation cobalt remains pseudo-octahedral; afterwards it binds as monomers or within basic-acetate clusters. Subsequent hydroxide reaction releases acetate and leaves substitutional Co in ZnO.',8,'IV.B and Scheme 1',['nucleation-model'],'model_context','author_interpretation'),
('geometry-barrier','Why dopants inhibit nucleation','The authors propose that Co-containing basic-acetate precursors face an additional octahedral-to-tetrahedral reorganization barrier and reduced driving force for crystallization. The absence of pre-nucleation tetrahedral Co supports this account, but no activation barrier or intermediate concentration is independently measured.',8,'IV.B',['nucleation-model'],'model_context','author_interpretation'),
('solubility-model','Why doped particles are smaller','Higher metastable bandgap energies and smaller TEM sizes are interpreted as a thermodynamic limitation, rather than solely slower kinetics. Surface or near-surface dopants are proposed to strain the lattice, raise specific surface energy and solubility, and shift the critical nucleus radius upward. Under the reported conditions only impurity-free nuclei survive.',8,'IV.B continuing on p. 9',['nucleation-model'],'model_context','author_interpretation'),
('equation3','Equation 3: curvature-dependent solubility','The source’s Gibbs–Thompson relation is S_r = S_B exp[2σV_m/(rRT)]. Here r is crystallite radius, S_B bulk solubility, V_m molar volume and σ specific surface energy. The relation provides the author’s strain/solubility rationale; no fitted σ, S_B or critical-radius value is supplied.',8,'Equation 3',['nucleation-model'],'model_context','author_model'),
('cleaning-rationale','Why remove exposed dopants','Heating in TOPO is interpreted to solvate and remove surface-bound ions, leaving internally doped particles. It offers a processing alternative to the earlier epitaxial undoped-ZnO shell procedure. The source claims somewhat better resolved spectra after TOPO cleaning, without providing a quantitative universal purity threshold.',9,'IV.C',['surface-cleaning-control','topo-co','topo-ni'],'model_context','author_interpretation'),
('lf-localization','Ligand-field localization and homogeneity','The close match of Co fine structure to bulk energies is used to argue that ligand-field states remain localized and weakly affected by nanocrystal confinement. Narrow Ni lines likewise support a relatively homogeneous environment. The source has inconsistent Co term labels: ⁴T₁(P) in its principal assignment and ⁴T₂(P) at some later passages; the reader does not silently relabel the original text.',9,'IV.D.i and p. 7 blue-band assignment',['mcd','optical-absorption'],'curator_interpretation','source_evidence_conflict'),
('sum-rule','MCD intensity-sum interpretation','Co ligand-field MCD is intense and pseudo-A-like, with a small net negative intensity attributed to out-of-state spin–orbit mixing involving ⁴T₂(F). Ni has a net positive intensity attributed to strong ground-term spin–orbit coupling. These assignments explain sum-rule deviations; no independent wavefunction reconstruction is supplied.',9,'IV.D.i',['mcd-intensity-model'],'model_context','author_interpretation'),
('equation1','Equation 1: C-term intensity normalization','C₀/D₀ = (kT/βH)(Δε/ε). The source uses k = 0.69503 cm⁻¹ K⁻¹ and β = 0.46686 cm⁻¹ T⁻¹, T in kelvin and H in tesla; it calls Δε molar ellipticity and ε the molar absorption coefficient. Table 1 ratios are corrected for saturation effects. This normalization is a source-defined analysis, not a synthesis condition.',5,'Equation 1 and definitions',['mcd-intensity-model'],'model_context','author_model'),
('equation2','Equation 2: B-term intensity normalization','B₀/D₀ = (1/βH)(Δε/ε) describes the temperature-independent B-term ratio. It lacks the kT factor in equation 1. The original formula and labels are retained so Co C-term and Ni B-term ratios remain scientifically distinct.',5,'Equation 2',['mcd-intensity-model'],'model_context','author_model'),
('ni-ct','Nickel ligand-to-metal charge transfer','The broad Ni sub-bandgap absorption is discussed around 18 000–23 000 cm⁻¹, alongside the broader regional window stated earlier. By comparison with polarized bulk spectra and Ni:ZnS literature, the source assigns Ni²⁺ as acceptor and the valence band as donor, producing Ni⁺ and a bound valence-band hole. This differs from ligand-field excitation.',10,'IV.D.ii',['charge-transfer-model'],'model_context','author_interpretation'),
('equation4','Equation 4: charge-transfer state','The original expression is Ni²⁺ + hν → Ni⁺ + h⁺_VB. The associated state is described as a central Ni⁺ ion with an electrostatically bound, hydrogen-like valence-band hole. It is an excited-state interpretation, not chemical reduction during synthesis.',10,'Equation 4',['charge-transfer-model'],'model_context','author_model'),
('co-ct-assignment','Cobalt charge-transfer assignment and alternatives','The Co negative MCD intensity approaching 25 200 cm⁻¹ is too intense for a spin-forbidden ligand-field feature and has a comparatively small C₀/D₀ ratio. The authors favor LMCT by comparison with Ni and an electronegativity model, while acknowledging that prior thin-film work assigned MLCT and that overlapping transitions may coexist.',10,'IV.D.ii',['charge-transfer-model'],'model_context','author_interpretation'),
('equation5','Equation 5: optical electronegativity','E_LMCT = 30 000 cm⁻¹[χ_opt(L) − χ_opt(M)] + ΔSPE + 10Dq. The cited Jørgensen/Lever framework combines donor–acceptor electronegativity difference, change in spin-pairing energy and the tetrahedral ligand-field contribution. It is a semiempirical interpretation, not a newly measured equation of state.',10,'Equation 5',['charge-transfer-model'],'model_context','author_model'),
('equation6','Equations 6a–b: spin-pairing terms','SPE = [⟨S(S+1)⟩ − S(S+1)]D and D = (7/6)[(5/2)B + C]. B and C are Racah electron-repulsion parameters. This D belongs to spin-pairing energy and must not be confused with the distinct zero-field-splitting D used in the magnetic discussion.',10,'Equations 6a and 6b',['charge-transfer-model'],'model_context','author_model'),
('ct-parameters','Ligand-field parameters used in the CT model','For Ni, the source uses Dq = 420 cm⁻¹, B = 770 cm⁻¹ and C/B = 4.8, giving ΔSPE = +(2/3)D = 4370 cm⁻¹. For Co it uses Dq = 390 cm⁻¹, B = 775 cm⁻¹ and C/B = 4.5, giving ΔSPE = +(4/3)D = 8440 cm⁻¹. These model parameters are not separate synthesis batches.',10,'IV.D.ii model parameters',['charge-transfer-model'],'model_context','author_model'),
('electronegativity-parameters','Optical-electronegativity estimates','The source takes χ_opt(Ni²⁺) ≈ 2.0 and χ_opt(Co²⁺) ≈ 1.9, a difference of 0.1. It compares tetrahalide complexes with Cl⁻, Br⁻ or I⁻, where Ni charge transfer is roughly 6600 cm⁻¹ lower. Note 33 cites molecular O²⁻ values of 3.0–3.9, typically 3.2. These are cited chemical contexts, not additional materials synthesized here.',10,'IV.D.ii and note 33',['charge-transfer-model'],'cited_context','literature_model_parameters'),
('ct-prediction','Model comparison of Ni and Co transitions','The model predicts a Co–Ni LMCT difference near 6770 cm⁻¹. Taking the Ni onset as approximately 19 500 cm⁻¹, defined where CT intensity equals the ligand-field maximum, predicts approximately 26 300 cm⁻¹ for Co. Its agreement with the observed Co lower-limit feature near 25 200 cm⁻¹ supports, but does not uniquely prove, the LMCT assignment.',10,'IV.D.ii prediction',['charge-transfer-model'],'model_context','author_derived_estimate'),
('oxide-electronegativity','Participation of oxide ligands in ZnO bands','The inferred effective χ_opt of dopant-bound ZnO oxide is approximately 2.4, compared with about 3.2 for typical molecular oxide and about 2.0 for pure ZnO lattice oxide. The authors infer easier oxidation by more than 2 eV relative to a molecular oxyanion and partial local polarization by the dopant.',11,'IV.D.ii',['charge-transfer-model'],'model_context','author_derived_interpretation'),
('confinement-electronegativity','Predicted confinement sensitivity','Using empirical ZnO band-edge behavior, the authors estimate that accessible confinement could increase the effective oxide electronegativity by only approximately 0.02 relative to bulk; local polarization may reduce this further. This small predicted effect is consistent with nearly unchanged ligand-field fine structure, rather than a directly measured χ-versus-size series.',11,'IV.D.ii',['charge-transfer-model'],'model_context','author_derived_estimate'),
('equation7','Equations 7a–b: donor–acceptor mixing','The model writes ψ′_A = √(1−c²)ψ_A − cψ_D, with c approximately −⟨ψ_A|H|ψ_D⟩/(E_A−E_D). CT intensity scales with c²; mixing depends on the resonance integral and inverse donor–acceptor ionization-energy mismatch. The original bra-ket notation is retained in the equation crop.',11,'Equations 7a–b',['charge-transfer-model'],'model_context','author_model'),
('ct-intensity','Why LMCT is expected to dominate','Cited theory places valence-band p–d hybridization more than an order of magnitude above conduction-band s–d mixing in II–VI materials, favoring LMCT intensity. A very weak negative visible Co MCD signal with the same S = 3/2 saturation is tentatively assigned as MLCT, with roughly one-tenth the intensity. This is explicitly a hypothesis.',11,'CT intensity discussion',['charge-transfer-model'],'model_context','author_hypothesis'),
('ct-wording-conflict','Weak-CT donor and acceptor wording','The weak-Co-feature paragraph calls it Co-to-valence-band MLCT but subsequently describes a conduction-band acceptor. The reader preserves this internal source wording conflict and the tentative nature of the assignment; it does not repair the electronic-state identity as a confirmed measurement.',11,'Weak visible MCD paragraph',['charge-transfer-model'],'curator_interpretation','source_evidence_conflict'),
('giant-zeeman','Why the band edge responds to dopant magnetization','The Co bandgap MCD follows the S = 3/2 magnetization of isolated Co dopants, which the authors interpret as exchange-induced semiconductor magnetization. The large excitonic response motivates magneto-optical applications but is distinct from the aggregate’s room-temperature ferromagnetic loop.',11,'IV.D.iii',['exchange-model'],'model_context','author_interpretation'),
('equation8','Equations 8a–b: mean-field exchange','The model uses ΔE_Zeeman(CB) = xN₀α⟨S_z⟩ and ΔE_Zeeman(VB) = xN₀β⟨S_z⟩, with x the dopant mole fraction and ⟨S_z⟩ the spin expectation value. N₀α is fixed to the typical +0.2 eV (+1600 cm⁻¹); it is not independently fitted from these data.',11,'Equations 8a–b',['exchange-model'],'model_context','author_model'),
('valence-model','Wurtzite valence-level approximation','Figure 9a is a calculated one-electron splitting pattern for H parallel to z. The source uses an approximately 56 cm⁻¹ zero-field wurtzite splitting, with Γ₉ ±3/2 levels and Γ₇ ±1/2 levels responding at different strengths, the latter one-third as much within the approximation. These are model levels, not extra measured spectral peaks.',11,'IV.D.iii continuing p. 12',['exchange-model'],'model_context','author_model'),
('co-exchange','Fitted Co exchange constant','The mean-field model gives N₀β = −2.3 ± 0.3 eV, also stated as −18 500 ± 2400 cm⁻¹, and an effective g near 200 for the Γ₉ doublet. Including fixed literature anisotropies gives a similar exchange magnitude but a poorer fit; solving all extra parameters is judged unwarranted. The uncertainty and approximation remain attached to the estimate.',12,'IV.D.iii and Table 2',['exchange-model'],'model_context','author_derived_fit'),
('volume-scaling','Limits of volume-only predictions','The fitted Co value is smaller in magnitude than the approximately −5 eV prediction from inverse-cell-volume scaling. The source argues that such scaling captures overlap but omits ligand electronegativity and confinement. It is a comparison of model assumptions, not a revision to the measured Figure 9 shifts.',12,'IV.D.iii',['exchange-model'],'model_context','author_interpretation'),
('equation9','Equation 9: microscopic exchange estimate','N₀β_hyb ≈ −(1/S)[⟨ψ_VB|Ĥ_pd|ψ_t₂⟩²/E_LMCT]. The numerator describes valence-band–dopant mixing; small ZnO cell dimensions increase overlap, whereas relatively high LMCT energy offsets it. The equation is a configuration-interaction approximation with no new atomistic calculation supplied.',12,'Equation 9',['exchange-model'],'model_context','author_model'),
('ni-exchange','Estimated Ni exchange despite weaker MCD','Assuming comparable Co/Ni resonance integrals, using S = 1 versus 3/2 and LMCT energies near 19 500 versus 25 200 cm⁻¹ gives a Ni exchange magnitude about 1.9 times Co, N₀β ≈ −4.5 ± 0.6 eV (−36 300 cm⁻¹). This is model-derived, while the measured Ni Zeeman/MCD response is at least tenfold smaller because its ground state responds differently to field.',12,'IV.D.iii and abstract',['exchange-model'],'model_context','author_derived_estimate'),
('table2-comparators','Table 2: external bulk comparisons','The table lists bulk Co values for CdTe −2.33, CdSe −2.12 and ZnTe −3.03 eV from reference 39; bulk Mn values are CdTe −0.83, CdSe −1.30, ZnTe −1.1 and ZnO −2.7 eV from reference 40. Only Co:ZnO −2.3 ± 0.3 and Ni:ZnO −4.5 ± 0.6 eV are this paper’s nanocrystal values. Blank cells remain unreported, not zero.',12,'Table 2 and footnote',['exchange-model'],'cited_context','literature_comparison'),
('co-ground-state','Co ground-state magnetization','The tetrahedral Co ⁴A₂ ground state lacks orbital angular momentum. Out-of-state spin–orbit mixing, mainly with ⁴T₁(F), gives a small positive splitting 2D = +5.5 cm⁻¹, used to explain nesting in Figure 8a. Dilute frozen solutions show isolated S = 3/2 behavior rather than collective ordering.',12,'IV.D.iv',['magnetization-model'],'model_context','author_interpretation'),
('ni-ground-state','Ni ground-state magnetization','First-order spin–orbit and trigonal fields split the Ni ³T₁(F) ground term into an A₁ (J = 0) ground level and A₂/E excited levels around 160/260 cm⁻¹. The cited bulk estimates are accurate to roughly ±25 cm⁻¹. Field-induced mixing gives a second-order Zeeman/B-term response and little ground-state ⟨S_z⟩, explaining weaker band shifts despite larger estimated N₀β.',12,'IV.D.iv',['magnetization-model'],'model_context','literature_based_author_model'),
('bterm-wording','Temperature dependence wording','The results call Ni B₀/D₀ temperature independent and Figure 8b nearly temperature independent below about 40 K, whereas the discussion describes temperature-dependent B-term intensity. The reader retains the reported temperature window and distinguishes low-temperature behavior from the broader model discussion.',12,'IV.D.iv and p. 6 results',['magnetization-model'],'curator_interpretation','source_wording_scope'),
('aggregation-interpretation','Author interpretation of aggregate ferromagnetism','Free-standing Co:ZnO colloids are described as paramagnetic; aggregation produces rapid low-field saturation and hysteresis. The authors associate the transition with substitutional dopants rather than phase segregation, citing unchanged diffraction and the absence of post-aggregation annealing. This remains the source’s conclusion, with the actual microscopy, spectroscopy and measurement limits visible.',13,'IV.D.v',['magnetization-model'],'model_context','author_interpretation'),
('equation10','Equation 10: magnetic relaxation','The model writes τ = τ₀ exp(KV/kT), where KV is the magnetocrystalline anisotropy barrier and τ₀ is an inverse attempt frequency of order 10⁻⁹ s. Larger domains can retain magnetization longer; neither K nor a specimen-specific relaxation time is directly measured here.',13,'Equation 10',['magnetization-model'],'model_context','author_model'),
('domain-estimate','Estimated lower bound on magnetic domain size','A Brillouin-function analysis of the 300 K saturation curvature suggests effective S at least approximately 8000, requiring at least about 5000 Co ions. Using 3.6% Co and 4.9 nm particles, the authors estimate about 200 Co per nanocrystal and a minimum domain near 25 nanocrystals. These linked model estimates are lower bounds and averages, not direct spatial counts.',13,'IV.D.v',['magnetization-model'],'model_context','author_derived_estimate'),
('interface-hypothesis','Proposed role of fusion interfaces','The authors propose that aggregation both increases domain size and introduces interface defects supplying carriers for double-exchange, RKKY or Zener coupling. Free-standing particles are paramagnetic rather than superparamagnetic, motivating an additional coupling mechanism. Interface defect density and the operative exchange mechanism are not established experimentally here.',13,'IV.D.v',['magnetization-model'],'model_context','author_hypothesis'),
('outlook','Proposed future architectures','The conclusion proposes processable DMS quantum dots as building blocks for more sophisticated ferromagnetic semiconductor architectures. Planned experiments would test interface-defect and weak charge-transfer assignments. Device performance, carrier density, electrical spin injection and those follow-up outcomes are not measured in the supplied source.',14,'Conclusion',['source-context'],'cited_context','source_outlook'),
])
more('colloid-rationale',13,'Conclusion');more('solubility-model',9,'Critical nucleus continuation');more('lf-localization',7,'Printed ⁴T₂(P) assignment');more('co-exchange',6,'Figure 9b');more('ct-wording-conflict',11,'Acceptor description');more('ni-ground-state',13,'Second-order Zeeman continuation')
rows('sources',[
('identity','Article identity','Dana A. Schwartz, Nick S. Norberg, Quyen P. Nguyen, Jason M. Parker and Daniel R. Gamelin, Magnetic Quantum Dots: Synthesis, Spectroscopy, and Magnetism of Co²⁺- and Ni²⁺-Doped ZnO Nanocrystals. Journal of the American Chemical Society 2003, 125(43), 13205–13218, DOI 10.1021/ja036811v. Received June 20, 2003; published on the web October 1, 2003.',1,'Title, byline and footer',['source-context'],'source_metadata','verified_source_identity'),
('affiliation','Affiliation and source metadata','The source lists the Department of Chemistry, University of Washington, Seattle, with Daniel R. Gamelin as corresponding author. PDF download and creation metadata are administrative and do not supersede the printed publication year.',1,'Byline and metadata',['source-context'],'source_metadata','source_metadata'),
('si-identity','Matched supporting information','The main declaration lists six figures, S1 through S6. All are present across the four supplied SI pages, in the declared order and on the declared kinetics, cleaning, microscopy, luminescence and optical-comparison subjects. The SI author metadata identifies Daniel Gamelin and its title is Microsoft Word - SUPPINF.postrev; the pages themselves do not print the full DOI or article title.',14,'Supporting Information declaration',['source-context'],'source_metadata','verified_source_identity'),
('coverage','Complete supplied-source coverage','All fourteen main pages and four matched SI pages were read and visually inspected, including eleven main figures, six SI figures, Scheme 1, two tables and all ten equation numbers with thirteen labeled components. All 51 numbered reference/note entries are retained for traceability. Original curves and images are preserved without reconstruction.',14,'Complete supplied source',['source-context'],'source_metadata','reviewed_source_coverage'),
('sample-boundaries','Specimen and concentration boundaries','Initial 2% feed titrations, approximately 5% as-grown Co TEM particles, 1.7% Co/5 nm frozen solution, 1.5% Ni/4 nm film, 3.6% Co aggregate, deliberately surface-bound Co, SI Ni microscopy, 2% Co luminescence, SI 5% TOPO comparison and 4.2 nm pure ZnO comparison remain distinct cohorts. Similar chemistry alone does not establish a common physical batch.',5,'Figures 5–10 and SI S1–S6',['source-context'],'curator_interpretation','curation_scope'),
('missing-fields','Preparation and raw-data limits','Several dopant-specific masses, solvent and washing volumes, separation settings, ramp rates, vessel specifications, isolated yields and storage periods are unreported. Raw spectra, individual-particle data and full magnetic sweep logs are not supplied. No unknown condition is filled from a figure appearance or an uninspected reference.',2,'Sample preparation and full source',['source-context'],'curator_interpretation','reviewed_source_limitation'),
('reference-scope','Scope of cited literature','All 51 numbered references and notes are represented, including multiple works within a single number. They provide contextual provenance only: external documents have not been independently read as part of this source review. Bulk comparison values and prior core/shell or ethanol protocols do not create newly verified synthesis data points here.',1,'References across the article',['source-context'],'cited_context','external_reference_scope'),
('funding','Acknowledgments and measurement provenance','The article acknowledges NSF DMR-0239325 and ECS-0224138, Research Corporation RI0832, ACS-PRF 37502-G, Semiconductor Research Corporation 2002-RJ-1051G and UW RRF-2651. Norberg acknowledges a UW/PNNL fellowship. TEM was collected at EMSL/PNNL with assistance from Chongmin Wang; these are administrative and facility-provenance facts.',14,'Acknowledgment',['source-context'],'source_metadata','source_metadata'),
])
for n in range(1,5):more('si-identity',n,f'SI page S-{n}, matching declared figure subjects','si')
items['motivation']['text'] += ' The cited prior device materials typically have ordering temperatures at or below approximately 110 K; that is not a measured limit for this paper’s aggregates.'
items['sum-rule']['text'] += ' The Co pseudo-A response is described as dominated by four spinors of the split ⁴T₁(P) term, with signs resembling CoCl₄²⁻ and other tetrahedral or pseudotetrahedral Co compounds.'
items['aggregate-phase']['text'] += ' Related Ni-aggregate TEM is cited as forthcoming in reference 50; it is not among the supplied current images.'
items['co-isosbestic']['text'] += ' The later-synthesis bandgap-intensity trend extrapolates approximately to the origin, which the authors interpret as most added base entering ZnO and only a small fraction remaining in molecular precursors; no exact cluster fraction is measured.'
items['affiliation']['text'] += ' The ACS copyright, price, contact details and manuscript code JA036811V are bibliographic information rather than experimental instructions.'
items['co-color']['text'] += ' The discussion assigns starting-ion bands to ⁴T₁g(F)→⁴T₁g(P) and ⁴T₁g(F)→⁴A₂g, with one or more DMSO molecules proposed in the pseudo-octahedral coordination sphere.'
more('co-color',7,'IV.B starting cobalt coordination')
items['equation5']['text'] += ' The optical electronegativity refers to nonbonding e orbitals; the proposed CT electron enters a σ-antibonding t₂ set lying 10Dq higher, motivating the added ligand-field term.'
items['giant-zeeman']['text'] += ' The source describes conduction-band exchange as largely Coulombic and valence-band exchange as strong, direct antiferromagnetic coupling arising from oxo–dopant covalency; its p–d hybridization contribution dominates N₀β relative to N₀α.'
